# What gets uploaded to Vercel. The site serves exactly two things: the page and
# the functions — plus the package those functions import. Everything else here is
# dev-only, and without this file the whole repo root is published as static files
# (dev.py, the test suite, the parsers, the screenshots) because Vercel serves the
# project directory as-is.
#
# This is the same allowlist rule dev.py enforces locally, so what you check in
# the emulator is what the deployment actually exposes.
tests/
scripts/
docs/
dev.py
requirements-dev.txt
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
.vercel/

# Runtime state. This is the directory that holds the SQLite database, the
# generated encryption and auth key files, and every stored object. A build
# on a machine that has run Studio locally would otherwise publish all of it
# as static files, readable at /var/studio/.studio-encryption-key and
# /var/studio/studio.db from the deployment's URL. The encryption key opens
# every sealed provider credential in that database, so this line is the
# difference between a deployment and a disclosure.
var/

# The local environment files. .studio_env.sh holds both generated keys in
# plaintext, and prompt-studio.env names the database and every model key
# slot. They are working copies, never content.
.studio_env.sh
prompt-studio.env

# Anything holding a real credential. The template is published on purpose —
# it is the documented list of variables, with placeholders only — but a local
# .env must never be. Vercel serves the project directory as-is, so without
# this an .env on the machine that ran `vercel build` would be downloadable
# from the deployment's URL.
.env*
!.env.example

# The Python version pin. The platform reads it to choose the runtime, so it
# has to be in the upload. It is a one-line file with nothing in it.
.python-version

# pyproject.toml and uv.lock are NOT excluded, and that is deliberate.
#
# The platform installs a function's dependencies from uv.lock when it is
# present, so these two files are the deployment's dependency list, not build
# artifacts. Excluding them was right when the lock declared nothing; now it
# declares cryptography, psycopg and psycopg-pool, which the code imports and
# without which the deployed function cannot store a credential or open a
# Postgres connection. They are tracked in git for the same reason.

# The notes and the containers. AUDIT.md, CHANGELOG.md, Dockerfile and
# docker-compose.yml describe the project rather than being it.
AUDIT.md
CHANGELOG.md
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml

# Session scratch. These are produced by an interactive shell and by the
# scripts in scripts/; none of them is content.
out.txt
smoke_out.txt
*.out.txt
vbuild.txt
start_studio.bat
git_status.txt
git_log.txt
git_branch.txt
git_remote.txt
git_porcelain.txt
gitignore_diff.txt
audit_studio.txt
audit_tests.txt
audit_web.txt

# A stray directory named "undefined" is what a path built from an undefined
# string looks like. It is empty, and it is not a project directory.
undefined/
storage/
